      <floats-group id="article-22091.s12">
        <fig id="article-22091.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Posterior Musculature of the Lower Leg. This illustration displays a posterior view of the leg with the gastrocnemius heads reflected to reveal the underlying soleus and plantaris muscles. Labels identify the popliteal fossa, the calcaneal tendon, and the various tendons of the deep flexor group as they course toward the medial malleolus. The relationship between the biceps femoris and semitendinosus muscles is also demonstrated at the superior aspect of the field. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Gray+Anatomy+439&#x00026;title=Special%3AMediaSearch&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray438" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22091.s13">
        <fig id="article-22091.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Structural Components of the Triceps Surae. This diagram highlights the relationship between the medial and lateral heads of the gastrocnemius and the underlying soleus muscle. The plantaris is shown traversing the posterior leg to its distal insertion point. Identification of the calcaneus establishes the mechanical leverage point for plantar flexion. Contributed by OpenStax College, 2017. (Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 license.)</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Figure__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22091.s14">
        <fig id="article-22091.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Surface Anatomy of the Posterior Leg Compartment. This image highlights the muscular definition of the calf during resisted exercise. Labels specify the anatomical positions of the gastrocnemius heads relative to the calcaneal tendon. This perspective allows for the assessment of muscular symmetry and the integrity of the posterior compartment landmarks. Contributed by Bruno Bordoni, PhD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gastrocnemius" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
